+This section supersedes all conflicting instructions below, including pale backing fields, reduced-motion preferences, session-once intros, sound and quality controls. Historical briefs remain below for context.
+
+Build the spider in actual 3D first. Define its body, eight legs, depth and engraved X before deriving the matching logo. Use the same black-metal character in the entrance, site identity and reusable assets. Render on transparent backgrounds with controlled edge light; never add a white plaque to the website to display it. The entrance descends on silk to the centre, settles with its back X visible and reveals only EVEN to form XEVEN.
+
+The website is silent and always uses full motion. Remove sound, motion and quality settings. Play the intro on every fresh index mount or reload; keep Skip, Escape and Replay so entry remains usable.
+
+Preserve the preferred original console artwork. Begin with it on the right of the first panel. As the visitor scrolls, enlarge its actual display until that screen covers the whole viewport. Present the remaining homepage chapters inside that display. Before the footer, return the console to physical scale, slide it into a side dock and display the new spider plus XEVEN. Keep the console parked while a surface grows from its screen to become the footer. Keep the journey continuous and reversible, with ordinary document scrolling.
+
+Make route changes feel like switching desktop app windows. Apply the same transition language to the offline edition while preserving real file links, queries and browser history. Keep the existing product information and page roles. Deliver the updated existing Site and a fresh complete ready-to-open ZIP containing code, all viewing assets, the 3D model, transparent logo masters, licenses and rebuild notes. Distinguish build/DOM/geometry validation from unavailable actual browser/GPU testing.
+
+---
